# WCAG 2.2 AA Compliance Rules

## 1. Images and Media Accessibility

### Images must have alt text

- All `<img>` elements MUST have meaningful `alt` attributes
- If decorative, use `alt=""` (empty alt text)
- For complex images, provide detailed descriptions via `aria-describedby`
- SVG icons MUST have `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` when conveying meaning
- Background images with content MUST have text alternatives

### Media accessibility

- Videos MUST have captions and transcripts
- Audio content MUST have transcripts
- Auto-playing media MUST have controls and be pausable

## 2. Color and Contrast

### Color contrast requirements

- Normal text: minimum 4.5:1 contrast ratio
- Large text (18pt+ or 14pt+ bold): minimum 3:1 contrast ratio
- UI components and graphics: minimum 3:1 contrast ratio
- Never rely on color alone to convey information

### Focus indicators

- All interactive elements MUST have visible focus indicators
- Focus indicators MUST meet 3:1 contrast ratio against adjacent colors
- Focus indicators MUST be at least 2px thick or have equivalent area

## 3. Keyboard Navigation

### Keyboard accessibility

- All interactive elements MUST be keyboard accessible
- Tab order MUST be logical and follow visual flow
- Skip links MUST be provided for main content areas
- Keyboard traps MUST be avoided (except in modals with proper escape)
- Custom interactive components MUST support standard keyboard patterns

### Focus management

- Focus MUST be managed when content changes dynamically
- Modal dialogs MUST trap focus within the modal
- Focus MUST return to appropriate element when modals close
- Never remove focus without moving it somewhere else

### Focus visibility (WCAG 2.2)

- Focus MUST not be entirely hidden by author-created content
- When focus is partially obscured, the focused area MUST be visible
- Focus indicators MUST not be completely covered by other content

## 4. ARIA and Semantic HTML

### Semantic HTML

- Use proper heading hierarchy (h1 → h2 → h3, no skipping levels)
- Use semantic elements (`nav`, `main`, `article`, `section`, `aside`)
- Form elements MUST have associated labels
- Use `button` elements for actions, `a` elements for navigation

### ARIA attributes

- Use ARIA labels when visible text is insufficient
- `aria-expanded` for collapsible content
- `aria-current` for current page/step indicators
- `aria-live` regions for dynamic content updates
- `aria-describedby` for additional descriptions
- Landmark roles when semantic HTML isn't sufficient

### ARIA best practices

- Don't change semantic meaning with ARIA
- Ensure ARIA attributes have valid values
- Test with screen readers
- Prefer semantic HTML over ARIA when possible

## 5. Forms Accessibility

### Form labels and instructions

- All form inputs MUST have associated labels
- Use `<label>` elements or `aria-labelledby`
- Required fields MUST be clearly indicated
- Provide clear instructions before form fields
- Error messages MUST be associated with fields using `aria-describedby`

### Error handling

- Errors MUST be announced to screen readers
- Error messages MUST be clear and actionable
- Focus MUST move to first error field on submission
- Use `aria-invalid` attribute for fields with errors

### Authentication accessibility (WCAG 2.2)

- Provide alternative authentication methods that don't rely on cognitive function tests
- Allow password managers and paste functionality
- Avoid memory-based authentication (unless alternative methods exist)
- Support biometric authentication where available
- Don't require users to transcribe characters they cannot distinguish

### Redundant entry prevention (WCAG 2.2)

- Auto-populate previously entered information when safe to do so
- Provide options to reuse information within a session
- Don't require users to re-enter identical information unnecessarily
- Exception: Re-entry may be required for security or data verification

## 6. Interactive Elements

### Links and buttons

- Link text MUST be descriptive (avoid "click here", "read more")
- External links SHOULD be indicated
- Buttons MUST have accessible names
- Icon-only buttons MUST have `aria-label`
- Ensure adequate touch target size (44x44px minimum)

### Custom components

- Complex widgets MUST follow ARIA authoring practices
- Provide keyboard support matching native elements
- Announce state changes to screen readers
- Implement proper ARIA roles and properties

### Drag and drop interactions (WCAG 2.2)

- All functionality that uses dragging movements MUST have an alternative single-pointer method
- Provide keyboard alternatives for drag-and-drop operations
- Consider users with mobility impairments who cannot perform dragging gestures

## 7. Content Structure

### Headings and landmarks

- Page MUST have exactly one h1
- Heading levels MUST not skip (h1 → h2 → h3)
- Use headings to create content outline
- Implement landmark regions (header, nav, main, aside, footer)

### Lists and tables

- Use proper list markup for groups of related items
- Tables MUST have headers (`<th>`) with appropriate scope
- Complex tables MUST have captions and summaries
- Data tables MUST use `scope` attributes

## 8. Dynamic Content

### Live regions

- Use `aria-live` for status messages and updates
- Set appropriate politeness level (polite/assertive)
- Ensure dynamic content changes are announced
- Provide loading states for async content

### Route changes (Next.js specific)

- Announce route changes to screen readers
- Move focus appropriately on navigation
- Update page title on route change
- Consider focus management for single-page app behavior

## 9. Mobile and Responsive Accessibility

### Touch targets (WCAG 2.2 Updated)

- Interactive elements MUST be at least 24x24px (WCAG 2.2 minimum)
- Target spacing of at least 24px between targets is recommended
- For better usability, maintain 44x44px targets when possible
- Ensure adequate spacing between touch targets
- Support both touch and mouse interactions
- Exception: Targets in sentences or text blocks may be smaller

### Responsive design

- Ensure content is accessible at all viewport sizes
- Text MUST be readable without horizontal scrolling
- All functionality MUST be available on mobile devices

### Fixed reference points (WCAG 2.2)

- When content requires specific spatial positioning, provide fixed reference points
- Ensure users can orient themselves when content changes or scrolls
- Provide consistent navigation and landmarks across viewport changes

## 10. Performance and Accessibility

### Loading and timeouts

- Provide progress indicators for loading content
- Allow users to extend time limits
- Provide pause/stop controls for auto-updating content
- Ensure core functionality works without JavaScript

## Implementation Guidelines

### Testing requirements

- Test with keyboard navigation only
- Test with screen reader (VoiceOver, NVDA, JAWS)
- Validate HTML markup
- Run automated accessibility tests (axe-core with WCAG 2.2 rules)
- Verify color contrast ratios
- Test focus visibility and management
- Test drag-and-drop alternatives
- Verify touch target sizes and spacing
- Test authentication flows for accessibility
- Test on mobile devices

### Code review checklist

- [ ] All images have appropriate alt text
- [ ] Form elements have labels
- [ ] Heading hierarchy is correct
- [ ] Interactive elements are keyboard accessible
- [ ] Focus indicators are visible and not obscured
- [ ] Color contrast meets requirements
- [ ] ARIA attributes are used correctly
- [ ] Error messages are accessible
- [ ] Dynamic content updates are announced
- [ ] Touch targets meet minimum 24x24px size
- [ ] Drag-and-drop has keyboard alternatives
- [ ] Authentication doesn't rely solely on cognitive tests
- [ ] Redundant data entry is minimized
